---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTABlock from '../components/CTABlock.astro';

const milestones = [
  { year: '2005', note: 'First job. Ecole hôtelière de Lausanne. Joined as an intern. Left a manager, seven years later. The institution took hospitality seriously – and that left a mark.' },
  { year: '2012', note: 'Six months at Parker Hannifin\'s European HQ. eBusiness marketing across 25 countries. First taste of how a Fortune 500 actually moves – and where the gap sits between HQ and the field.' },
  { year: '2013', note: 'Joined Aspediens as Head of Marketing. 30 people on day one. 100+ the day we were acquired.' },
  { year: '2016', note: 'First exit. Aspediens acquired by CSC/DXC. Suddenly colleagues on every continent and a Fortune 500 brand to integrate. The work shifted to building bridges – between teams, countries, and two very different ways of doing business.' },
  { year: '2017', note: 'Detoured through FELFEL. A passion for a physical product, designed and shipped into real offices. A different kind of growth education.' },
  { year: '2020', note: 'Joined Locatee post Series A, just as the world stopped going to offices. Workplace analytics, perfect timing in the worst way. We figured it out together.' },
  { year: '2024', note: 'Second exit. Big learnings. Locatee acquired by Tango. We restructured the company and initiated an M&A process under pressure. I sat at the table with our co-CEOs, CFO, board, and the acquirer – while holding the team together on the other side.' },
  { year: '2025', note: 'SVP Marketing at Tango. Came in through the acquisition, took the team over, ended up on the executive leadership team of the company that bought us. PE-backed, US-headquartered. Combining American ambition with European execution every day.' },
  { year: '2026', note: 'Stepping into fractional CEO, advisor, and coaching work. Fewer companies. Deeper with each.' },
];

const principles = [
  {
    title: 'Clarity before cleverness.',
    body: 'I would rather say the obvious thing first and be useful than say the interesting thing first and be impressive. Most founders are drowning in clever. They need a person who will tell them the obvious, out loud, once.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Story is strategy.',
    body: 'Every organization runs on a story. When the story is clear, the org chart feels like decoration. When the story fractures, no amount of process can paper over it. Most of my work is making the story honest again.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Two identities, one person.',
    body: 'I hold the operator and the human at the same time. The spreadsheet and the late-night conversation. People work with me because both show up. Not because I hide one to seem more serious.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Less, better.',
    body: 'I take on a small number of companies. I say no often. The work only compounds when there is room in my week to think about it, and yours, properly.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Stève Cattin"
  description="Fifteen years in B2B tech, two acquisitions, and the long road that led to fractional CEO, executive advisor, and coaching work."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="about-hero container">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="about-hero__title">
      Fifteen years in tech.<br />
      Still asking the <span class="accent">obvious</span> question.
    </h1>
    <p class="about-hero__lede">
      I'm Stève. I help founders and senior leaders see the thing they can't
      quite name yet. Marketer by training. Advisor in practice. Storyteller
      wherever I'm allowed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- The long version -->
  <section class="about-body container-prose section-tight">
    <h2>The long version.</h2>

    <p>
      Here's what fifteen years in B2B tech and two acquisitions will teach
      you: when something isn't working, it's rarely just the plan. And it's
      rarely just the team. It's usually the space between them – where
      clarity breaks down, where ownership gets fuzzy, where smart people
      produce the wrong results anyway.
    </p>

    <p>
      I've spent my career working in those in-between spaces. Between the
      people building features and the customers paying for benefits – which
      is often the most expensive translation no one budgets for. Between
      American ambition and European execution. Between the strategy that
      makes sense on paper and the team that has to believe it enough to
      deliver it.
    </p>

    <p>
      I grew up in Switzerland between four languages and three cultures.
      That probably explains the affinity. I'm drawn to people who are
      translating between worlds – a technical founder learning to be a
      storyteller, a marketer learning to run a P&amp;L, a CEO trying to build
      a company that matches who they actually are, rather than who they
      thought they were supposed to be.
    </p>

    <p>
      I've done that work from the inside. Two acquisitions, both as part of
      the leadership team. From small-team Series A all the way to the
      executive leadership of a US-headquartered, PE-backed scale-up.
      Marketing, operations, people. Boards and executives, and the engineer
      who keeps everything running.
    </p>

    <p>
      The titles changed. The work underneath stayed the same: notice what
      isn't being said. Translate it. Make it possible for everyone in the
      room to move.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I work this way now.</h2>

    <p>
      The obvious next step after an SVP seat is another SVP seat. I looked
      at it honestly and realised the part of the work that gives me energy –
      rather than spending it – is the quiet conversation with a leader
      about what's really going on. Not the dashboard. The thing underneath.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the work is shifting.
    </p>

    <p>
      Today I sit with founders and CEOs whose teams are stuck and who can't
      quite name why. Where the strategy looks fine on paper, the hires are
      good, and yet things aren't landing. The fix is rarely the plan.
    </p>

    <p>
      With board members and investors whose portfolio companies are
      talented, well-funded, and somehow still slow. Sometimes the right
      intervention is a fractional operator. Sometimes it's an honest
      sparring partner for the founder. Figuring out which is part of the
      work.
    </p>

    <p>
      And with operators who sense that the hardest problem they're carrying
      is the one inside themselves. With them I work as a coach.
    </p>

    <p>
      Fewer companies than I could take. More depth with each.
    </p>

    <p>
      I take my time. I read what I'm given carefully – the deck, the org
      chart, the body language, the things people say after the meeting. I
      ask before I prescribe. Thorough by nature, thoughtful by training.
      And I tend to notice when someone in the room hasn't spoken yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      If any of that resonates, the door is open.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Timeline -->
  <section class="about-timeline section container">
    <header class="about-timeline__header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Path</p>
      <h2>Twenty years, compressed.</h2>
    </header>

    <ol class="about-timeline__list">
      {milestones.map((m) => (
        <li class="about-timeline__item">
          <span class="about-timeline__year">{m.year}</span>
          <p class="about-timeline__note">{m.note}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <!-- Principles -->
  <section class="about-principles section container">
    <header class="about-principles__header">
      <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
      <h2>Four things I try not to forget.</h2>
    </header>

    <div class="about-principles__grid">
      {principles.map((p, i) => (
        <article class="about-principles__card">
          <span class="about-principles__num">№ {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h3>{p.title}</h3>
          <p>{p.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <!-- The other thing I'm building (coda) -->
  <section class="about-body container-prose section-tight">
    <h2>The other thing I'm building.</h2>

    <p>
      There is a place – a house, really – that we are slowly walking toward.
      Not a pivot. Not a plan B. The road and the horizon, at the same time.
    </p>

    <p>
      It will be in the south. Stone walls, a long table, a garden with more
      pollinators than people. The work will be hospitality, but only on the
      surface. Underneath, it's the same thing I've been doing for fifteen
      years – making a room where people can put their armor down, tell their
      stories, and find what they need for their next chapter.
    </p>

    <p>
      And one part is not negotiable. The doors open for young people who
      weren't welcomed elsewhere, and for artists to create.
    </p>

    <p>
      A house, a home, a table.
    </p>

    <p>
      There's a longer version of this, if you want it: <a href="/blog/a-long-table-in-the-south">A long table in the south →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <CTABlock
    id="book"
    eyebrow="If this resonates"
    headline="Let's have the conversation before anything else."
    body="A twenty-minute intro call. No deck, no pre-work. I'll ask what's actually going on. If the fit is real, we'll both know by the end."
    ctaLabel="Book an intro call"
    ctaHref="mailto:hello@stevecattin.com?subject=Intro%20call"
    secondaryLabel="Read some writing first"
    secondaryHref="/blog"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* -------- Hero -------- */
  .about-hero {
    padding-block: clamp(4rem, 4vw + 3rem, 7rem);
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .about-hero .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-hero__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-hero);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  .about-hero__title .accent {
    color: var(--color-cobalt);
  }

  .about-hero__lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--color-graphite);
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  /* -------- Body -------- */
  .about-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .about-body h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

  .about-body p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: var(--lh-prose);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* -------- Timeline -------- */
  .about-timeline__header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
    max-width: 680px;
  }

  .about-timeline__header h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .about-timeline__list {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .about-timeline__item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .about-timeline__item:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }

  .about-timeline__year {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-cobalt);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-mono);
  }

  .about-timeline__note {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  /* -------- Principles -------- */
  .about-principles__header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-10);
    max-width: 680px;
  }

  .about-principles__header h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .about-principles__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-principles__card {
    background: var(--color-white);
    border: 0.5px solid var(--color-line);
    padding: var(--space-8);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .about-principles__num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-mono-sm);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-cobalt);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .about-principles__card h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .about-principles__card p {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--color-graphite);
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .about-timeline__item {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-2);
    }
    .about-principles__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
